A||N||7||G.Mig||Idle help (Emissions)||||9
Z||000000||G.Mig||10-20-1999||06:38 AM||||My 84 4cylinder, just failed my local emissions test. It idle's to high in park,<BR>but it's just fine in drive. I have taken it<BR>to Midas and a couple of other exhaust &<BR>emission shops but they can't figure it out.<BR>I have had to take it to another mechanic<BR>last year when I live in another state to <BR>adjust the idle speed for me in order for the<BR>car to past the test. Can anyone tell me a simple way to adjust the idle, I have checked<BR>the Haynes book but to no avail.<BR>Thanks||||reg||9
Z||000001||fierospeeder||10-21-1999||06:53 PM||||My car one year didn't pass emissions.  My egr valve was getting stuck.  Another thing was bad spark plug wires.  I replaced both of these and passed with zero hydrocarbons, i took it another year with higher octane it didn't do as well, i know octane shouldn't matter.  Also, you can check your TPS sensor, if you have a multimeter, get the readings, i dont have the exact numbers available to me right now.  Mine was doing the same thing in rpms.  I changed my TPS in my tbird and i noticed when i let go of the throttle and coast the idle speed drops to around 1000, instead of my higher number.<BR>i can get you the readings for the 2.5 but i dont think i got them for the 2.8.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000002||G.Mig||10-22-1999||01:02 AM||||Thanks for your reply, I will give your suggestions a try. If you can send me the readings for the 2.5 that will be great.<P>G.Mig||||reg||7
Z||000003||fierospeeder||10-22-1999||01:25 AM||||my manual says at closed throttle position, the output of the tps is low(approximately .5 volts).  As the throttle plate opens, the output voltage should be approximately 5 volts.  It says the 2.5 tps is nonadjustable.  I think theres three wires going to the TPS, i havn't done this for a while so im basing it on my service manual.<BR>you should poke into the darkblue wire  and the black wire is the ground coming out of the TPS.  The best thing you can do is just to see if its within that range, with the key on, and just move the accelerator cable by hand to see what it is at full throttle.  ||||reg||1
Z||000004||theogre||10-22-1999||02:50 PM||||I know I say this allot.... if the TPS has never, or not recently, been replaced then just replace it. These sensors do wear out. like yours is gone or on it's way to gone even is it appears to test ok.<P>check for vacuum leaks. they will throw off the idle. check the rubber elbows on the PCV line ends. the manifold end of that line tends to be crappy.||||reg||1
Z||000005||88 Fiero||10-22-1999||03:15 PM||||For the new members of this forum...take Ogre's advice...The man knows the car and been tinkering about it for awhile...||||reg||1
Z||000006||Cooter||10-23-1999||09:11 PM||||When I put a new valve cover gasket on my 2.5 today, I found that the gasket under the EGR valve had cracked and was a small vacuum leak. After this fix, the car idles at about 900 rpms instead of the 1200 like before.||||reg||10
Z||000007||Bill Osmer||10-24-1999||08:44 PM||||If I were to find a stock of TPSs (from the original mfrs, no less), would there be an interest from anyone in reimbursing me for them? And if so, which ones would be most interesting: 2.8L, 2.5L or 2.2L?||||reg||5
